---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import MouseDriftTool from '../../components/MouseDriftTool.astro';
import NewToolRelatedTests from '../../components/NewToolRelatedTests.astro';
import FAQ from '../../components/FAQ.astro';
import JsonLd from '../../components/seo/JsonLd.astro';
import { absoluteUrl } from '../../config';

const description = 'After Start, this page records pointermove movementX, movementY, and client position for a still period. The CSS-pixel figures are not a sensor fault verdict.';
const faq = [
  { question: 'Does a large total mean the sensor is damaged?', answer: 'No. The totals are movementX, movementY, and client-position change from pointermove events in the period you started. This page does not see the sensor, the mouse pad, or a driver. A large number, a zero, and a period with no events are descriptions of those events.' },
  { question: 'Why does a finished period sometimes say “Not provided”?', answer: 'Not provided means the cell had no finite value to show. Zero pointermove events leave the movement and position cells as not provided. A sample that arrived with movement 0 and 0 is different: those cells show 0 CSS px. A missing component is not filled in as zero.' },
  { question: 'Why can the movement sum differ from the position change?', answer: 'The movement sum adds movementX and movementY from the stored samples. The position change subtracts the first client position from the last. The browser can omit a coordinate, coalesce steps, or send a movement component that does not match the two viewport positions. This page shows both figures when it can calculate them and does not force them to match.' },
  { question: 'Does this page test controller stick drift?', answer: 'No. Stick position is a gamepad axis. This page records pointermove only and does not call the Gamepad API. The Controller Test reads gamepad buttons and axes. If a controller is moving the operating-system pointer, the events here are still pointer events.' },
  { question: 'Which events are stored?', answer: 'pointermove events that arrive after the 3-second countdown and before the selected 5, 10, or 15 second period ends. Events before Start, during the countdown, and after the period are ignored. When the browser provides a non-empty coalesced-event list for a pointermove, those coalesced events are the stored samples and the parent event is not added again.' },
];
const related = [
  { href: '/mouse-test', title: 'Mouse Test', description: 'Check buttons, movement, and wheel input.', icon: '◉' },
  { href: '/mouse-polling-rate-test', title: 'Polling Rate Test', description: 'Estimate how often pointer events arrive.', icon: '⌁' },
  { href: '/mouse-dpi-test', title: 'Mouse DPI Test', description: 'Divide a measured path by a distance you enter.', icon: '⌖' },
  { href: '/double-click-test', title: 'Double Click Test', description: 'Look at unexpected repeat clicks.', icon: '⠿' },
  { href: '/controller-test', title: 'Controller Test', description: 'Read gamepad axes, which this page does not.', icon: '▣' },
];
const schema = [
  { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebApplication', name: 'Stationary Mouse Cursor Drift Test', url: absoluteUrl('/mouse-drift-test'), applicationCategory: 'UtilitiesApplication', operatingSystem: 'Any', offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' } },
  { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage', mainEntity: faq.map((item) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: item.question, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.answer } })) },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Stationary Mouse Cursor Drift Test" {description} path="/mouse-drift-test">
  <JsonLd data={schema} />
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Mouse Drift Test' }]} />
  <section class="page-hero shell compact">
    <h1>Stationary Mouse Cursor Drift Test</h1>
    <p>Press Start, leave the pointer still, and read the movement this browser reports during the timed period.</p>
  </section>
  <MouseDriftTool />
  <article class="content">
    <h2>How to use this stationary mouse cursor drift test</h2>
    <p>Press Start. A 3-second countdown runs first. The countdown does not store movement, so you can let go of the mouse, trackpad, or other pointing device before the sample begins. When the status line says the period is recording, leave the pointer still until that line says the period ended. The recording length is the button you selected: 5, 10, or 15 seconds. Ten seconds is selected when the page loads. Change it before Start. The buttons do not change the length of a countdown or a recording that is already running.</p>
    <p>The pointer can rest anywhere inside this browser window. The plot is not a target you have to stay on. This page listens for pointermove on the window. A pointer outside the window is not visible to the page, and a pointermove that never arrives is not invented. The cells update during the recording and stop when the period ends. Reset test clears the samples, the plot, and a countdown or recording that is still running. Pressing Start after a finished period begins a new countdown and drops the previous samples. The page keeps only the latest period. It does not average periods, and it does not keep them after you leave the site.</p>
    <p>The plot draws client positions relative to the first recorded position. The center of the box is that first position. One CSS pixel of change is drawn as two pixels on the plot. A point that does not fit is drawn on the edge, and the net-position cell keeps the unclamped numbers. Before any position is recorded, the crosshair is only a guide in the center of the box. If no recorded event included both clientX and clientY, the plot has no points and the net-position cell says not provided.</p>

    <h2>Movement X and movement Y are the event components</h2>
    <p>Each stored sample reads movementX and movementY from a pointermove event, or from a coalesced event the browser attached to that pointermove. The <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/uievents/#dom-mouseevent-movementx" rel="noopener noreferrer">UI Events specification</a> defines movementX as the horizontal component of the pointer movement since the last pointermove, and movementY as the vertical component. The unit is CSS pixels. Positive movementX is to the right. Positive movementY is down. The Movement X cell adds the horizontal components. The Movement Y cell adds the vertical ones. A sample of 1.5 to the right and a later sample of 0.5 to the left produce a Movement X sum of 1. That sum is the components that arrived. It is not a distance measured with a ruler on the desk.</p>
    <p>A sample joins those sums only when both components are finite numbers. Zero is finite. A sample whose movementX is 0 and whose movementY is 0 adds nothing to either sum, and it still counts as a pointermove sample. The cells then show 0 CSS px. A missing component, or a component that is not a finite number, leaves that sample out of the sums. The status line reports how many samples were left out. The page does not treat the missing component as zero, because a written zero would look like a still pointer. If every sample is left out, the movement cells say not provided even though the event count is greater than zero.</p>
    <p>The step-length sum adds the length of each included sample. The length is the square root of movementX squared plus movementY squared. A sample of 3 CSS pixels right and 4 CSS pixels down has a step length of 5. The largest step is the greatest of those lengths. The step-length sum can be larger than the straight-line change when the pointer moves away and comes back. Consider two samples: movement (3, 4) at client position (10, 20), then movement (0, -2) at client position (10, 18). Movement X is 3. Movement Y is 4 plus -2, which is 2. The step lengths are 5 and 2, so the step-length sum is 7 and the largest step is 5. The client Y change is 18 minus 20, which is -2, and the client X change is 0. The page is built to show that disagreement. It does not replace the movement sum with the position change, and it does not add the two figures together.</p>
    <p>Printed numbers keep at most four digits after the decimal point. A value whose absolute size is under 0.00005 CSS pixels is printed as 0. The rounding is only the way the cell is written. CSS pixels are the unit on the event. On a screen whose device pixel ratio is above 1, one CSS pixel can cover more than one physical pixel. This page does not multiply by devicePixelRatio, and it does not convert CSS pixels into millimeters. A conversion would need a length the page does not measure.</p>

    <h2>Client position change is a different figure</h2>
    <p>clientX and clientY are the pointer position in CSS pixels relative to the viewport. The net position change subtracts the first recorded position from the last recorded position. It uses only samples that include both coordinates as finite numbers. One such sample produces a change of 0, 0, and the cell adds the words “one position,” because a single point has no later point to compare. No such sample produces not provided.</p>
    <p>The movement sums and the net position change answer different questions. The sums add movement components. The net change compares two viewport positions. They can disagree even when both are present. The <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/pointerevents/#dom-pointerevent-getcoalescedevents" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pointer Events specification</a> lets a browser coalesce several movements into one pointermove and expose the smaller events through getCoalescedEvents. The last event in that list is the dispatched pointermove. When the list is non-empty, this page stores those coalesced events and does not add the parent event a second time. When the list is missing or empty, the page stores the pointermove itself. A pointermove without a coalesced list may already combine several steps, and the page cannot recover steps the browser did not include.</p>
    <p>Scrolling, a pointer that leaves the window and comes back, or a coordinate the event did not carry can also separate the movement sum from the position change. Quote the figure you have. The page listens for pointermove only. It does not also listen for pointerrawupdate or mousemove. Those extra listeners would count one motion more than once on browsers that fire more than one of them for the same movement.</p>

    <h2>A still pointer can produce no pointermove</h2>
    <p>The period can end with zero pointermove samples. The event cell then reads 0, and the movement and position cells say not provided. That result is not a measured zero. It means this page did not receive a pointermove during the recording. The pointermove event is how the page observes a coordinate change. If the browser does not dispatch one, there is no sample to add. A pointer that was left outside the window is in that same outcome, because events outside the window do not reach this listener. The countdown and the length buttons are on the page, so a pointer moved out before recording starts is not observed.</p>
    <p>A run of samples that really contain movement 0 and 0 is a different result. Those samples did arrive, and the movement cells show 0 CSS px. The page does not promote a missing event into that zero, and it does not promote a zero into a grade. The status line for a finished period repeats the same cells in one sentence so a copied line matches the grid.</p>

    <h2>What this page does not conclude</h2>
    <p>Nothing in the cells is a verdict about a sensor. A large total does not mean a sensor is damaged. A total of zero does not mean a device is healthy. The operating system, a pointer-speed setting, and the browser sit between the device and this script. The page cannot read those settings, so it does not subtract them. A hand still touching the mouse, a desk that moves, or a pointer-speed setting can change the CSS pixels. The page cannot name which of those happened. It can repeat the events it stored. It does not inspect a sensor lens, a mouse pad, a driver version, or a firmware log, and it does not recommend cleaning, replacement, or a repair.</p>
    <p>The plot has the same limit. A line in the box is the recorded positions, drawn from the first client position. It is not a picture of a hand, and it has no pass line. A point drawn on the edge was clamped only in the drawing. The net-position numbers stay exact, aside from the four-digit rounding used in the cell.</p>
    <p>This page also does not read a gamepad. Controller stick input is an axis on a gamepad object, as described by the <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/gamepad/#dom-gamepad-axes" rel="noopener noreferrer">Gamepad specification</a>. The <a href="/controller-test">Controller Test</a> shows the buttons and axes the browser exposes for a connected gamepad. This page never calls that API. If a controller is driving the operating-system pointer, the events here are still pointer events. Use the controller page for the stick. Use this page for pointermove during a still period.</p>
    <p>The <a href="/mouse-test">Mouse Test</a> checks buttons, wheel input, and whether the pointer is moving. It does not keep a timed still-period total. The <a href="/mouse-polling-rate-test">Polling Rate Test</a> estimates how often pointer events arrive. The <a href="/mouse-dpi-test">Mouse DPI Test</a> divides a path length by a distance you measure on the desk. This page does not ask for that distance and does not compute DPI. The <a href="/double-click-test">Double Click Test</a> looks at repeated clicks. A click during this recording is not stored as a click. Only pointermove is stored. The guide <a href="/guides/how-to-test-a-mouse">How to Test a Mouse</a> walks through buttons, scrolling, and input timing. It does not record a still period.</p>
    <p>Run the period again when you want another sample. Reset test, press Start, and wait out the countdown before you compare the two status lines. Two periods can differ because the pointer was not equally still, because the browser coalesced different steps, or because the pointer left the window. Compare the sentences the page printed. Do not turn either sentence into a hardware ranking.</p>
    <NewToolRelatedTests tests={related} />
    <FAQ items={faq} />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
